#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221101 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f5aefa9-a83c-5203-8b73-c96b5f7df6bf" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Row of a Table Using the ABM 2-Step Method"}] :question/id #uuid "14669099-db40-5dd2-b4d5-4db016374643" :question/math-academy-id "e-21538" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = -x - y, \\qquad y\\left(-1\\right) = 0$\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $\\Delta x = 1$, and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the next rows of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n$\\Delta y = \\dfrac1{12}(5y'_p + 8y' - y'_{-1}) \\cdot \\Delta x$\n*where $y_p$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and $y'_{-1}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.*\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ |\n| $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ |  |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}Predictor &: & Δy_{p} &= \\frac{1}{2}(3y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx, \\\\ Corrector &: & Δy|= \\frac{1}{12}(5y_{p}^{′} + 8y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y' = f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y)$,\n- $y'_{-1} = f(x_{-1}, y_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point ($x_{-1}, y_{-1})$, and\n- $y'_p = f(x_\\text{new}, y_p)$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $(x_\\text{new}, y_p) = (x + \\Delta x, y + \\Delta y_p)$.\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in\n\n$$\ny\\left(0\\right) \\approx \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the $2$-step ABM method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= -x - y \\\\\n&=-0 - \\frac{1}{4} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nFrom the first row of the table, we see that the slope at the previous point\n\n$$\n(x_{-1}, y_{-1}) = \\left(-1, 0\\right)\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\ny'_{-1} = 1\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y_p$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy_{p} &= \\frac{1}{2}(3y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(3 \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4}) - 1) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&=-\\frac{7}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{7}{8}}$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{new} &= x + Δx = 0 + 1 = 1, \\\\\ny_{p} &= y + Δy_{p} = \\frac{1}{4} + (-\\frac{7}{8}) =-\\frac{5}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{7}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{5}{8}}$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p}^{′} &= -x_{new} - y_{p} \\\\\n&=-1 - (-\\frac{5}{8}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{3}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{7}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{5}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{3}{8}}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= \\frac{1}{12}(5y_{p}^{′} + 8y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{12}(5 \\cdot (-\\frac{3}{8}) + 8 \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4}) - 1) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{12} \\cdot (-\\frac{39}{8}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{13}{32}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{7}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{5}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{3}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{13}{32}}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = 1\n$$\n\nTo get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $\\Delta y$ to $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y = \\dfrac{1}{4} + \\left(-\\dfrac{13}{32}\\right) = -\\dfrac{5}{32}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{7}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{5}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{3}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{13}{32}}$ |\n| $1$ | $-\\dfrac{5}{32}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(1) \\approx \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{5}{32}}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221102 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "874de02f-c5be-54ed-8d66-a3b1f1f23c9a" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Table Using the Predictor Equation of the ABM 2-Step Method"}] :question/id #uuid "c736760f-414e-5ab2-adfa-c09acce10d5e" :question/math-academy-id "e-21539" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = 2x - 2y + 1, \\qquad y(0) = 0$\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $\\Delta x = \\dfrac14$, and that the first step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, fill the missing entries in the table below, where $y_p$ denotes the predictor at each step.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c\n$$\n\nthe predictor equation of the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method (ABM2) with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y_p = \\dfrac12(3y' - y'_{-1}) \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y' = f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y)$, and\n- $y'_{-1} = f(x_{-1}, y_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1})$.\n\nThe initially predicted new $y$-value is then given by\n\n$$\ny_p = y + \\Delta y_p\n$$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in\n\n$$\ny\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right) \\approx \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the ABM $2$-step method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= 2x - 2y + 1 \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}) - 2 \\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}) + 1 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{1}$ |  |  |\n\nFrom the table, we see that the slope at the previous point\n\n$$\n(x_{-1}, y_{-1}) = \\left(0, 0\\right)\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\ny'_{-1} = 1\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y_p$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy_{p} &= \\frac{1}{2}(3y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(3 \\cdot 1 - 1) \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8} \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\dfrac14$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{1}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ |  |\n\nHence, according to the predictor equation, the initially predicted new $y$-value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p} &= y + Δy_{p} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{1}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\dfrac14$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{1}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{\\dfrac{1}{2}}$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221105 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ecbd28c-9d5a-5802-8792-6ba97651ecd2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Corrector Term"}] :question/id #uuid "a185d6d7-e351-5f24-891a-aeb318cef584" :question/math-academy-id "e-21565" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = x + y + 1, \\qquad y\\left(-2\\right) = 0$\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $\\Delta x = \\dfrac14$, and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the second row of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n$\\Delta y = \\dfrac1{12}(5y'_p + 8y' - y'_{-1}) \\cdot \\Delta x$\n*where $y_p$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and $y'_{-1}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.*\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $-2$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $-\\dfrac74$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $-1$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c\n$$\n\nthe $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}Predictor &: & Δy_{p} &= \\frac{1}{2}(3y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx, \\\\ Corrector &: & Δy|= \\frac{1}{12}(5y_{p}^{′} + 8y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y' = f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y)$,\n- $y'_{-1} = f(x_{-1}, y_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point ($x_{-1}, y_{-1})$, and\n- $y'_p = f(x_\\text{new}, y_p)$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $(x_\\text{new}, y_p) = (x + \\Delta x, y + \\Delta y_p)$.\n\nLooking at the partially filled table, we note the following:\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the current point $(x, y) = \\left(-\\dfrac74, -\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)$ is $y' = -1$.\n- From the first row, the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}) = \\left(-2, 0\\right)$ is $y'_{-1} = -1$.\n- From the second row, the slope at the predicted new point $(x_\\text{new}, y_p)$ is $y'_p = -1$.\n\nNow, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= \\frac{1}{12}(5y_{p}^{′} + 8y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{12}(5 \\cdot (-1) + 8 \\cdot (-1) - (-1)) \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{48} \\cdot (-12) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $-2$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $-\\dfrac74$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $-1$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221106 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5a37e3d-f1ce-57f2-baef-c464a4f89ad4" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using The ABM 2-Step Method"}] :question/id #uuid "ab9a026d-ddb2-52e8-9f52-4fbcf9b482a6" :question/math-academy-id "e-21566" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = x - 2y + 6, \\qquad y(0) = 2$\nTo approximate the solution, one step of the Runge-Kutta method has already been computed, resulting in the following estimate:\n$y(1) \\approx 3$\nUse the $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton Method with one step to approximate $y(2)$.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n$\\Delta y = \\dfrac1{12}(5y'_p + 8y' - y'_{-1}) \\cdot \\Delta x$\n*where $y_p$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and $y'_{-1}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.*" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM $2$-step) Method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}Predictor &: & Δy_{p} &= \\frac{1}{2}(3y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx, \\\\ Corrector &: & Δy|= \\frac{1}{12}(5y_{p}^{′} + 8y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y' = f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y)$,\n- $y'_{-1} = f(x_{-1}, y_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point ($x_{-1}, y_{-1})$, and\n- $y'_p = f(x_\\text{new}, y_p)$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $(x_\\text{new}, y_p) = (x + \\Delta x, y + \\Delta y_p)$.\n\nBefore we start, let's add the initial condition data and Runge-Kutta step data to a table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $2$ |  | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2$, we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 2 - 1 = 1\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the values of $y'$ at each point according to the given rule:\n\n- The slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}) = (0,2)$ is\n$y_{-1}^{′}|= x_{-1} - 2y_{-1} + 6; = 0 - 2 \\cdot 2 + 6; = 2$.\n- The slope at the current point $(x, y) = (1,3)$ is\n$y^{′}|= x - 2y + 6; = 1 - 2 \\cdot 3 + 6; = 1$.\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y_p$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy_{p} &= \\frac{1}{2}(3y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(3 \\cdot 1 - 2) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{new} &= x + Δx = 1 + 1 = 2, \\\\\ny_{p} &= y + Δy_{p} = 3 + \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{7}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac72$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p}^{′} &= x_{new} - 2y_{p} + 6 \\\\\n&= 2 - 2 \\cdot \\frac{7}{2} + 6 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac72$ | $1$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= \\frac{1}{12}(5y_{p}^{′} + 8y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{12}(5 \\cdot 1 + 8 \\cdot 1 - 2) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{12} \\cdot 11 \\\\\n&= \\frac{11}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac72$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac{11}{12}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new}=2\n$$\n\nTo get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $\\Delta y$ to $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= y + Δy \\\\\n&= 3 + \\frac{11}{12} \\\\\n&= \\frac{47}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac72$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac{11}{12}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\dfrac{47}{12}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(2)\\approx\\dfrac{47}{12}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221870 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5a37e3d-f1ce-57f2-baef-c464a4f89ad4" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using The ABM 2-Step Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9281e9d4-5156-5935-a12f-0b31dcd7392e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220131} {:answer/id #uuid "96df4676-1a59-5b23-b501-7d8cd375d923" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220132} {:answer/id #uuid "fe671c20-9b4e-5823-9bf1-07edcf2c0006" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220133} {:answer/id #uuid "0660a54c-7f48-565d-8fa2-8de1d4581270" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220134} {:answer/id #uuid "6c68a91b-8fed-5446-8f59-3cb7f6c6f4a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0660a54c-7f48-565d-8fa2-8de1d4581270" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220134} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0e3d9dc-a07e-534c-8e5d-8a45d420ef7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220130}] :question/id #uuid "f1a505b5-5206-5aba-aeb7-87c9ff569a07" :question/math-academy-id "q-329310" :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nConsider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} = 3 + x - 2x^{2}y, y(-1) = 1$\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton Method. What entry should be placed in the cell indicated in the table below, where the first step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, if the step size is $Δx = 1$?\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y^{′}$ | $Δy_{p}$ | $y_{p}$ | $y_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $1$ | $$? |  |  |  |  |  |\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n$Δy = \\frac{1}{12}(5y_{p}^{′} + 8y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx$\n*where $y_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and $y_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.*" :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221873 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "874de02f-c5be-54ed-8d66-a3b1f1f23c9a" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Table Using the Predictor Equation of the ABM 2-Step Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "9bf6e1bf-ec8f-57eb-84d0-bafee6b228af" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220142} {:answer-field/id #uuid "9d11a531-2868-5de9-b3ce-34de7fa96e76" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220143} {:answer-field/id #uuid "daff4911-46c7-5e2c-bd81-7e51a88ea2bc" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220144}] :question/id #uuid "74629dbe-868c-5d08-adfe-776d31c19676" :question/math-academy-id "q-329474" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} = 2x - 3y + 6, y(-1) = 2$\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx = \\frac{1}{2}$, and that the first step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, fill the missing entries in the table below, where $y_{p}$ denotes the predictor at each step.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y^{′}$ | $Δy_{p}$ | $y_{p}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{27}{16}$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221894 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "874de02f-c5be-54ed-8d66-a3b1f1f23c9a" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Table Using the Predictor Equation of the ABM 2-Step Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "29d4fe07-8321-53bb-a779-4717e94e8ad4" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220312} {:answer-field/id #uuid "3a2259ed-37e6-595f-8841-1d7b4133de34" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220313} {:answer-field/id #uuid "54c8cc0a-d3c1-5695-9164-65ab873f01e1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220314}] :question/id #uuid "4bd5c73e-14a0-5ec3-b975-8bd0d7a8fc7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-33306